#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Stake this host's mining rewards to a finalizer. The one call here that spends.

    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --amount 100  stake 100 cTAZ
    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --all             stake every spendable reward but --keep
    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --all --dry-run   run every check, send nothing
    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --all --wait      wait for Staking Day instead of stopping
    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --all --yes       no question, for a timer
    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --all --batch 500 stakes of at most 500 cTAZ each
    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --auto            what a timer runs: --all --yes, and
                                                    "not now" is not a failure
    sudo crosslink-stake --to ADDRESS --timer install   stake by itself from now on (below)

ADDRESS is a finalizer address, `zfinv1` + 128 characters: the one the
finalizer's node logs at start ("finalizer address:"). Self-describing: the
node refuses one that does not verify. A finalizer need not be on the active
roster to be staked to (only the largest N vote), so the seat is reported,
never required.

For a node host that crosslink-ansible built with the headless wallet on
(`crosslink_disable_wallet: false`). The playbook installs it there and never
runs it. A node run by hand points it at its own files with the CROSSLINK_*
variables below. Standard library only.

Before it sends, it checks, and stops at the first check that fails:

  1. the wallet is on and has started, and the rewards are this wallet's:
     no miner_address, or miner_address is this wallet's own unified
     address (rewards shielded, below)
  2. the node is synced and on the network's chain: the last finalized
     block is in this node's chain, read from its own RPC (--reference URL
     also compares the block at an explorer's tip)
  3. no earlier stake from this script is still waiting for a block
  4. the address decodes
  5. Staking Day is open, with 15 blocks to spare, and the chain is past the
     first block a stake is valid at (v14: 20,736)
  6. the wallet has read the chain up to the tip: its notes
     (wallet_spendable_funds, the wallet's own tip) and the payout address
  7. the amount is there: the wallet's spendable shielded notes (its own
     rule, 3 confirmations) plus the payout address's rewards older than
     10 blocks

Rewards shielded. zebrad takes any address of the chain as miner_address,
and a unified address with an Orchard receiver is paid its reward as one
Ironwood note a block (v14 zebra-rpc, `new_coinbase`). The headless wallet's
own unified address is printed at start ("USER WALLET ADDRESS:") and answered
by wallet_spendable_funds; with miner_address set to it the rewards land in
this wallet as shielded notes, and the wallet builds every stake from its
Ironwood notes first and transparent coins after (wallet/src/lib.rs,
`stake_ironwood_to_finalizer`, src_pools), so such a stake has no transparent
input and no address the chain can read it by. The playbook sets it with
crosslink_miner_shielded. This script stakes either way, and both kinds of
reward count: a host that switched keeps what its payout address held.

It stakes in batches of at most --batch cTAZ (1,000 by default), one stake
at a time: the wallet pays a stake with the rewards one coin or note each (a
block's reward is one note), and a stake of 8,925 cTAZ in about 1,785 coins
was built and then refused by the node's own mempool. It asks once for the
whole amount, then before each stake after the first it waits until the one
before is in a block and the wallet has read that block, so no coin is
offered twice, and runs every check above again. A batch that stops leaves
the ones before it staked; run it again for the rest.

Every stake is a power of 10 zats: 1, 10, 100, 1,000 … cTAZ (0.1 and 0.01
too). Consensus asks it from block 55,296 of the Feature Net (v15's
FeaturenetUpgrade1), and the v15 wallet refuses any other amount at every
height, before that block too (wallet/src/lib.rs,
`stake_ironwood_to_finalizer`). Earlier builds take any amount, so such a
stake is valid on every build, and the script stakes no other kind: it does
not depend on reading which build it talks to. --all is staked as such
pieces, largest first, none over --batch and none under --min rounded down to
a power of 10 (50 → 10), and what is left under the smallest piece waits for
a later pass; a --amount that is not one of those is refused.

The wallet answers every refusal with the same words ("failed to create
staking transaction from notes") and prints its reason in the node's log; the
script reads that line back and says it, with what to do about it.

The reply is written to disk before anything reads it. v15's
wallet_staking_action answers with the transaction id; a build without it
(v14) takes the same request through staking_command, which answers with no
id, and then the id is read from the node's log, where the wallet prints the
stake it built. The wallet derives the bond key from the seed, and the script
finds the stake by its id in wallet_staking_positions once its block is read,
and writes the key to the ledger then. Every attempt, a failure too, is a line in the ledger. A timeout
is not a failure: the stake may still land, so it never sends again by
itself. A stake the wallet built is valid for 40 blocks; one not in a block
by then is given up, once the wallet has read that far, and the next run
stakes again. Every line names the chain (NETWORK in chain.env), and a line
from another chain — a host reset from one season to the next keeps its
ledger — is left alone, as is one sent at a block above this chain's tip.

Exit: 0 sent, or nothing to stake · 1 a check stopped it, or the node
refused · 3 no reply in time; the stake may still land.

Staking by itself. `--timer install` writes a systemd service and timer that
run this file with --auto every 30 minutes (--every), restarts the timer and
starts a pass at once (a changed address, minimum or script is in force in
this Staking Day, not the next tick's; a timer already as asked is left
alone), and `--timer remove` takes them away; `--timer status` shows the next run, the last one and the
last stake, `--timer show` prints the two units and changes nothing. Run as
root it is a system timer, otherwise a user timer (systemctl --user). The
units carry --to, --min, --keep, --batch, --reference and --dry-run as given
at install, and every CROSSLINK_* variable set then, so a node run by hand
installs it through the same wrapper it stakes with. Nothing but this file is
needed on the host. crosslink-ansible runs this same install on a host whose
inventory sets crosslink_autostake_to, and never stakes itself.

With --auto every check above still runs on every pass. A check that passes by
itself later (Staking Day closed, the node syncing, the wallet reading, an
earlier stake not in a block yet) ends the pass with exit 0; anything else is
exit 1 and the unit shows failed. --min is what keeps the bonds few: a pass
stakes only once that much has gathered (10 cTAZ unless given at install).
--keep is what stays behind for the fee: 1 cTAZ, where a stake's fee is
5,000 zats a coin it spends (ZIP-317), 0.01 cTAZ for a stake of 1,000.
Install it with --dry-run first to watch a Staking Day go by with nothing
sent. Before finality starts on a chain that begins as proof-of-work, only
--reference can see a fork.
"""
